      <div>
        <h2>Standby vs. Portable -- Which Do You Need?</h2>
        <p><strong>Whole-home standby generator</strong> (Generac Guardian, Kohler 20RESCL, Cummins QuietConnect):</p>
        <ul class="features-list">
          <li>Permanently installed on a concrete pad outside</li>
          <li>Automatically starts within 10-30 seconds of power loss</li>
          <li>Runs on natural gas or propane (no refueling)</li>
          <li>Sized to power whole home or essential circuits</li>
          <li>$8,000-$15,000 installed for typical 18-26kW units</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Portable generator with transfer switch:</strong></p>
        <ul class="features-list">
          <li>Lower upfront cost ($1,500-$3,500 transfer switch + $800-$2,500 unit)</li>
          <li>Requires manual setup (roll out, refuel, start, switch)</li>
          <li>Good for occasional outages or budget-conscious homeowners</li>
          <li>Doesn't auto-start</li>
        </ul>
        <p>For most homeowners on the Wasatch Front, we recommend the whole-home approach with a managed transfer switch. The cost difference is now smaller than it used to be, and homeowners overwhelmingly prefer "everything works" to "I have to think about what to run."</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
